  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 PM EDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nHigh pressure directly overhead is making for a very tranquil and \npleasant late August day! Highs are expected to reach the upper 70s \ntoday amid mostly sunny skies with light northwest winds. Partly \ncloudy skies overnight should keep any fog concerns at bay as \nradiational cooling should be somewhat limited, although lows are \nexpected to be in the low to mid 50s again Tuesday morning.\nWith high pressure situated over the Great Lakes region again \ntomorrow, highs will be slightly warmer in the upper 70s to low 80s \nas the airmass overhead moderates and winds shift to be out of the \nsouthwest. Comfortable humidity with dew points in the mid to upper \n50s continues through Tuesday.\n\nWednesday is increasingly warmer and humid with 40-60% chances for \nrain and storms. An upper level trough will deepen over the Great \nLakes and a surface cold front is expected to move through during \nthe day. Our area will be in the warm sector out ahead of the front \nwith increased WAA and low level moisture transport from southerly \nwinds. Given the favorable timeframe, highs in the low 80s, and \ndewpoints in the upper 60s to near 70, severe weather is possible as \n1500-2500 J/kg of surface based instability and decent 0-6 km shear \nof 25-30 kts should develop Wednesday afternoon and evening. With \nthe frontal passage during the peak heating hours of the day, at \nleast isolated severe weather will be possible as SPC maintains a \nMarginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for Wednesday. 60 mph wind gusts and 1\" \nhail will be the main threats with multicellular clusters that are \nexpected to develop but if a few discrete storms can maintain \nthemselves (low confidence), an all hazards setup may occur. \nForecast soundings from the NAM, RAP, and GFS show varying \namounts of low level shear and helicity, so there are still some\nuncertainties to sort through. In the vicinity of the cold \nfront, 100-150 m2/s2 of SRH which would provide enough spin to \nsupport an isolated tornado threat. All of the models have poor \nlapse mid and low level lapse rates though, which could indicate\nthis being a a limiting factor and/or a possible failure mode. \nThere is still uncertainty regarding exact timing, coverage, and\nintensity of storms.\n\nAnother area of high pressure builds across the Midwest and Great \nLakes late in the week, bringing a return to dry conditions at least \nthrough the first half of the upcoming weekend. Have kept low \nchances (20-30%) for rain and storms for Sunday. Temperatures will \nremain near normal with a gradual warming trend into the mid 80s for \nhighs by the end of August.",
